WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.153
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Cambridge exams.

2
00:00:05.453 --> 00:00:09.188
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.488 --> 00:00:18.595
Unit seventeen: C1 Advanced: Reading Cross-text and Gapped Tasks. Today you will handle long texts, cross-text matching and gapped sentences.

4
00:00:18.895 --> 00:00:21.373
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.914
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:26.750 --> 00:00:27.902
Cross-text.

7
00:00:28.917 --> 00:00:31.880
Cross-text means using two or more texts together.

8
00:00:32.483 --> 00:00:35.063
Part 6 is cross-text multiple matching.

9
00:00:35.650 --> 00:00:38.744
Your turn. Say: Cross-text.

10
00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:42.671
Cross-text.

11
00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:44.464
Stance.

12
00:00:45.450 --> 00:00:47.879
Stance means the position a writer takes.

13
00:00:48.483 --> 00:00:50.460
Identify the writer's stance.

14
00:00:51.050 --> 00:00:54.097
Your turn. Say: Stance.

15
00:00:56.933 --> 00:00:57.842
Stance.

16
00:00:58.583 --> 00:00:59.747
Implication.

17
00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:03.260
Implication means a meaning that is suggested.

18
00:01:03.850 --> 00:01:05.699
Understand the implication.

19
00:01:06.283 --> 00:01:09.423
Your turn. Say: Implication.

20
00:01:12.267 --> 00:01:13.430
Implication.

21
00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:15.359
Counterargument.

22
00:01:16.350 --> 00:01:19.069
Counterargument means an argument against a view.

23
00:01:19.683 --> 00:01:21.729
The writer presents a counterargument.

24
00:01:22.317 --> 00:01:25.329
Your turn. Say: Counterargument.

25
00:01:28.167 --> 00:01:29.748
Counterargument.

26
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:31.264
Tone.

27
00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.261
Tone means the attitude in writing.

28
00:01:34.850 --> 00:01:36.304
The tone is ironic.

29
00:01:36.917 --> 00:01:38.838
Your turn. Say: Tone.

30
00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:42.527
Tone.

31
00:01:43.283 --> 00:01:44.250
Ironic.

32
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:47.946
Ironic means saying the opposite of what is meant.

33
00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:49.969
The comment was ironic.

34
00:01:50.583 --> 00:01:53.062
Your turn. Say: Ironic.

35
00:01:55.900 --> 00:01:56.901
Ironic.

36
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.651
Rhetorical.

37
00:01:59.650 --> 00:02:02.683
Rhetorical means used for effect, not for an answer.

38
00:02:03.283 --> 00:02:04.877
A rhetorical question.

39
00:02:05.483 --> 00:02:08.182
Your turn. Say: Rhetorical.

40
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.023
Rhetorical.

41
00:02:12.783 --> 00:02:14.191
Cohesive device.

42
00:02:15.183 --> 00:02:18.065
Cohesive device means a word that links ideas.

43
00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.475
Spot the cohesive device.

44
00:02:21.083 --> 00:02:24.107
Your turn. Say: Cohesive device.

45
00:02:26.967 --> 00:02:28.386
Cohesive device.

46
00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:32.897
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:33.750 --> 00:02:36.005
Visal, what is new at C1 Reading?

48
00:02:37.117 --> 00:02:49.336
The texts are longer and more complex, and the questions test implied meaning, attitude and the writer's stance. In Part 6 you compare four short texts on the same topic.

49
00:02:50.450 --> 00:02:52.600
How do I do a cross-text task?

50
00:02:53.717 --> 00:03:04.264
Read each text and summarise each writer's view in a few words. Then compare the views, deciding where the writers agree or disagree.

51
00:03:05.350 --> 00:03:07.013
How do I recognise tone?

52
00:03:08.117 --> 00:03:21.079
Watch for ironic or rhetorical language, adjectives with strong feeling and hedging words like perhaps or arguably. The tone often shows what a writer really thinks.

53
00:03:22.233 --> 00:03:23.629
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:24.483 --> 00:03:28.584
Grammar focus: Strategy: summarise, compare, infer.

55
00:03:28.884 --> 00:03:30.083
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:30.783 --> 00:03:34.327
Writer A supports the plan, whereas Writer B opposes it.

57
00:03:35.217 --> 00:03:38.133
The word 'arguably' shows that the writer is cautious.

58
00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:45.384
Summarise each writer's view in a few words, compare them and look at the tone to understand stance and implication.

59
00:03:46.150 --> 00:03:50.114
Be careful! Don't say: I read the texts separately only.

60
00:03:52.067 --> 00:03:54.449
Say: I compare the texts.

61
00:03:54.749 --> 00:03:56.586
Cross-text needs comparison.

62
00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.971
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

63
00:04:00.767 --> 00:04:02.290
Comparing Texts.

64
00:04:02.590 --> 00:04:05.472
Dalin reads four short texts about city bikes.

65
00:04:06.467 --> 00:04:09.360
Writer A strongly supports a new cycle scheme.

66
00:04:10.367 --> 00:04:13.725
Writer B criticises it as expensive and unnecessary.

67
00:04:14.733 --> 00:04:18.184
Writer C uses the word arguably and seems cautious.

68
00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.629
Dalin compares them to answer the question.

69
00:04:22.733 --> 00:04:26.770
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

70
00:04:27.617 --> 00:04:29.581
Cross-text means using blank.

71
00:04:29.881 --> 00:04:34.301
A, no text. B, only pictures. C, more than one text.

72
00:04:37.867 --> 00:04:40.482
The answer is C: more than one text.

73
00:04:41.617 --> 00:04:44.023
'Arguably' shows the writer is blank.

74
00:04:44.323 --> 00:04:47.779
A, cautious. B, angry. C, bored.

75
00:04:51.333 --> 00:04:53.124
The answer is A: cautious.

76
00:04:54.283 --> 00:04:55.888
Writer B blank the scheme.

77
00:04:56.188 --> 00:05:00.306
A, ignores. B, criticises. C, praises.

78
00:05:03.867 --> 00:05:06.586
The answer is B: criticises.

79
00:05:07.750 --> 00:05:09.622
A rhetorical question blank.

80
00:05:09.922 --> 00:05:14.133
A, needs an answer. B, is wrong. C, is for effect.

81
00:05:17.667 --> 00:05:19.864
The answer is C: is for effect.

82
00:05:21.017 --> 00:05:22.784
Tone shows the writer's blank.

83
00:05:23.084 --> 00:05:26.366
A, attitude. B, name. C, age.

84
00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:31.934
The answer is A: attitude.

85
00:05:33.133 --> 00:05:36.111
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

86
00:05:36.950 --> 00:05:39.159
How do you approach cross-text tasks?

87
00:05:47.950 --> 00:05:49.485
Now listen to Visal.

88
00:05:49.785 --> 00:06:01.345
I read each text and write a short summary of the writer's view. Then I compare the views and look at the tone to understand the stance. I choose the answer that is supported by the evidence.

89
00:06:01.645 --> 00:06:03.447
Excellent answer, Visal!

90
00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:09.754
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and strategy: summarise, compare, infer.

91
00:06:10.054 --> 00:06:14.970
Today's value is critical thinking. Think carefully and give reasons for your opinions.

92
00:06:15.270 --> 00:06:17.830
See you in Unit eighteen. Goodbye!

93
00:06:18.130 --> 00:06:20.608
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

